{#
	What the operator is saying loudly enough that you cannot walk away from it
	(XIV-166, §8.16).

	**The other half of the notices feature, and deliberately not the same half.**
	A notice whose reach is `dashboard` is a card in `dashboard/widget/notices.html.twig`,
	read when somebody chooses to open their landing page. A notice whose reach is
	`every_page` is this: a band in the shell, above whatever they came here to do,
	on the record they are editing and the list they are filtering. The two sets
	are disjoint in the `WHERE` clause (`App\Registry\Entity\NoticeReach`), so the
	dashboard, which is the one page that draws both surfaces, cannot print the
	same announcement twice.

	## Where it sits and why

	Immediately under the top bar, inside the same `container page-shell` as the
	page, above the page's own heading. Three things were on the table:

	* **Pinned to the viewport** (`position: fixed` or `sticky`). Refused. A band
	  that never scrolls away is not "you cannot get away from it", it is "you
	  cannot use the page": it covers content permanently, it needs the shell to
	  know its height in order to offset anything, and on a phone it takes a
	  quarter of the viewport for the whole session. An operator asking for
	  attention has not asked for the product to be smaller.
	* **Inside `<main>`, above the page content.** Refused because every page
	  would have to remember to include it, which is forty-odd templates and a
	  forty-first that forgets.
	* **Here.** The shell already has exactly one place every signed-in page goes
	  through, `_topbar.html.twig`, so a page cannot fail to have this and a page
	  added next year gets it without being told.

	**It costs nothing on a load with nothing to say.** `chrome.pageNotices` is
	empty on every ordinary request, this file renders no element at all, and the
	document is byte for byte what it was before this ticket. It does push the page
	down when there *is* something, which is the point of the channel rather than a
	regression in it: compact, two or three lines, and only for as long as the
	notice runs, which an operator had to put an end date on before they could
	publish it.

	## Colour, and the word beside it

	`alert alert-{tone}`, with the tone from `notice_tone()` and the mapping
	written out in full there. Never `text-bg-*`: that helper pins a foreground
	against a fixed brand colour and Bootstrap does not redefine it under
	`[data-bs-theme=dark]`, so a band built from it is legible on a light page and
	wrong on a dark one. Alerts are built from `--bs-{tone}-text-emphasis`,
	`-bg-subtle` and `-border-subtle`, and all three of those *are* redefined
	(§5.26).

	**The priority is printed as a word as well as a colour**, which is the tenant
	list's rule and matters more here than there: somebody who cannot tell the
	amber band from the red one is exactly the person who most needs to be told
	which of the two this is. It is visible text rather than `visually-hidden`,
	because colour blindness is not a screen reader.

	## No dismiss button, and that is the decision rather than an omission

	`App\Registry\Entity\NoticeReach::isDismissible()` has the argument at length.
	The short version: a band a reader can switch off once and never see again is
	the dashboard channel with extra steps, and a band nobody can switch off is an
	operator holding somebody's product hostage. Only one of those two parties
	should be paying, and it is the one who chose the channel, so the reader gets
	no button and the operator cannot publish this without saying when it stops.
#}
{% if chrome.pageNotices is not empty %}
	<div class="container page-shell">
		{% for notice in chrome.pageNotices %}
			{#
				`role="status"` rather than `role="alert"` for all four tones. The
				band is in the document when the page loads rather than appearing
				during it, and `alert` is an assertive live region: it interrupts
				whatever a screen reader is currently saying, which on a page that
				has only just started being read means interrupting the page's own
				title. Polite is the correct register for something that was
				already here when you arrived.
			#}
			<div class="alert alert-{{ notice_tone(notice.priority) }} py-2 mb-3" role="status">
				<p class="small text-uppercase fw-semibold mb-1">{{ notice.priority.labelKey|trans }}</p>
				<p class="fw-semibold mb-1">{{ notice.title }}</p>
				{#
					`nl2br` over escaped text, which is the whole of the formatting
					on offer here as on the dashboard card. Markdown, links and HTML
					were all considered and refused when this feature was built: the
					moment a notice can contain a link it is a channel somebody can
					be phished through, and that is worse on this surface than on the
					dashboard, because this one appears on pages where a customer is
					in the middle of doing something.
				#}
				<p class="mb-1">{{ notice.body|nl2br }}</p>
				{#
					Who said it and when, the same two facts the dashboard card
					carries and for the same reason: they are most of the difference
					between an announcement and a popup. The author is a name copied
					onto the notice when it was published, because this instance
					cannot read the `operator` table at all (§4.4).
				#}
				<p class="small mb-0 opacity-75">
					<time datetime="{{ notice.publishedAt|date('c') }}">{{ notice.publishedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}</time>
					· {{ notice.authorLabel }}
				</p>
			</div>
		{% endfor %}
	</div>
{% endif %}
